1
00:00:04,879 --> 00:00:12,718
Some weeks passed without bringing any change. We waited for Wemmick, and he made no sign. If I

2
00:00:12,718 --> 00:00:17,597
had never known him out of Little Britain, and had never enjoyed the privilege of being on a familiar

3
00:00:17,597 --> 00:00:24,758
footing at the Castle, I might have doubted him; not so for a moment, knowing him as I did.

4
00:00:24,758 --> 00:00:30,277
My worldly affairs began to wear a gloomy appearance, and I was pressed for money by

5
00:00:30,277 --> 00:00:37,160
more than one creditor. Even I myself began to know the want of money (I mean of ready

6
00:00:37,160 --> 00:00:42,796
money in my own pocket), and to relieve it by converting some easily spared articles of

7
00:00:42,796 --> 00:00:48,078
jewelery into cash. But I had quite determined that it would be a heartless fraud to take more

8
00:00:48,078 --> 00:00:53,277
money from my patron in the existing state of my uncertain thoughts and plans. Therefore,

9
00:00:53,277 --> 00:00:57,839
I had sent him the unopened pocket-book by Herbert, to hold in his own keeping,

10
00:00:57,839 --> 00:01:01,917
and I felt a kind of satisfaction—whether it was a false kind or a true,

11
00:01:01,917 --> 00:01:08,597
I hardly know—in not having profited by his generosity since his revelation of himself.

12
00:01:08,597 --> 00:01:14,597
As the time wore on, an impression settled heavily upon me that Estella was married.

13
00:01:14,597 --> 00:01:20,641
Fearful of having it confirmed, though it was all but a conviction, I avoided the newspapers,

14
00:01:20,641 --> 00:01:26,000
and begged Herbert (to whom I had confided the circumstances of our last interview)

15
00:01:26,000 --> 00:01:32,917
never to speak of her to me. Why I hoarded up this last wretched little rag of the robe of

16
00:01:32,917 --> 00:01:38,957
hope that was rent and given to the winds, how do I know? Why did you who read this,

17
00:01:38,957 --> 00:01:44,917
commit that not dissimilar inconsistency of your own last year, last month, last week?

18
00:01:44,917 --> 00:01:51,957
It was an unhappy life that I lived; and its one dominant anxiety, towering over all its other

19
00:01:51,957 --> 00:01:58,917
anxieties, like a high mountain above a range of mountains, never disappeared from my view. Still,

20
00:01:58,917 --> 00:02:06,078
no new cause for fear arose. Let me start from my bed as I would, with the terror fresh upon me

21
00:02:06,078 --> 00:02:11,519
that he was discovered; let me sit listening, as I would with dread, for Herbert’s returning step

22
00:02:11,519 --> 00:02:18,237
at night, lest it should be fleeter than ordinary, and winged with evil news,—for all that, and much

23
00:02:18,237 --> 00:02:25,678
more to like purpose, the round of things went on. Condemned to inaction and a state of constant

24
00:02:25,680 --> 00:02:35,680
restlessness and suspense, I rowed about in my boat, and waited, waited, waited, as I best could.

25
00:02:35,680 --> 00:02:39,559
There were states of the tide when, having been down the river, I could not get back through the

26
00:02:39,559 --> 00:02:46,000
eddy-chafed arches and starlings of old London Bridge; then, I left my boat at a wharf near the

27
00:02:46,000 --> 00:02:52,277
Custom House, to be brought up afterwards to the Temple stairs. I was not averse to doing this,

28
00:02:52,277 --> 00:02:58,237
as it served to make me and my boat a commoner incident among the water-side people there.

29
00:02:58,237 --> 00:03:02,319
From this slight occasion sprang two meetings that I have now to tell of.

30
00:03:02,319 --> 00:03:05,758
One afternoon, late in the month of February, I came ashore at the

31
00:03:05,758 --> 00:03:10,319
wharf at dusk. I had pulled down as far as Greenwich with the ebb tide,

32
00:03:10,319 --> 00:03:14,639
and had turned with the tide. It had been a fine bright day,

33
00:03:14,641 --> 00:03:20,839
but had become foggy as the sun dropped, and I had had to feel my way back among the shipping,

34
00:03:20,839 --> 00:03:27,839
pretty carefully. Both in going and returning, I had seen the signal in his window, All well.

35
00:03:27,839 --> 00:03:33,839
As it was a raw evening, and I was cold, I thought I would comfort myself with dinner at once;

36
00:03:33,839 --> 00:03:38,839
and as I had hours of dejection and solitude before me if I went home to the Temple,

37
00:03:38,839 --> 00:03:45,199
I thought I would afterwards go to the play. The theatre where Mr. Wopsle had achieved his

38
00:03:45,199 --> 00:03:51,078
questionable triumph was in that water-side neighbourhood (it is nowhere now), and to that

39
00:03:51,078 --> 00:03:57,519
theatre I resolved to go. I was aware that Mr. Wopsle had not succeeded in reviving the Drama,

40
00:03:57,519 --> 00:04:02,957
but, on the contrary, had rather partaken of its decline. He had been ominously heard of,

41
00:04:02,957 --> 00:04:08,436
through the play-bills, as a faithful Black, in connection with a little girl of noble birth,

42
00:04:08,438 --> 00:04:14,160
and a monkey. And Herbert had seen him as a predatory Tartar of comic propensities,

43
00:04:14,160 --> 00:04:19,917
with a face like a red brick, and an outrageous hat all over bells.

44
00:04:19,917 --> 00:04:25,319
I dined at what Herbert and I used to call a geographical chop-house, where there were maps

45
00:04:25,319 --> 00:04:31,398
of the world in porter-pot rims on every half-yard of the tablecloths, and charts of gravy on every

46
00:04:31,398 --> 00:04:36,597
one of the knives,—to this day there is scarcely a single chop-house within the Lord Mayor’s

47
00:04:36,597 --> 00:04:43,277
dominions which is not geographical,—and wore out the time in dozing over crumbs, staring at gas,

48
00:04:43,277 --> 00:04:50,639
and baking in a hot blast of dinners. By and by, I roused myself, and went to the play.

49
00:04:50,641 --> 00:04:55,718
There, I found a virtuous boatswain in His Majesty’s service,—a most excellent man,

50
00:04:55,718 --> 00:04:59,358
though I could have wished his trousers not quite so tight in some places,

51
00:04:59,358 --> 00:05:04,796
and not quite so loose in others,—who knocked all the little men’s hats over their eyes,

52
00:05:04,796 --> 00:05:09,559
though he was very generous and brave, and who wouldn’t hear of anybody’s paying taxes,

53
00:05:09,559 --> 00:05:16,480
though he was very patriotic. He had a bag of money in his pocket, like a pudding in the cloth,

54
00:05:16,480 --> 00:05:22,319
and on that property married a young person in bed-furniture, with great rejoicings;

55
00:05:22,319 --> 00:05:28,199
the whole population of Portsmouth (nine in number at the last census) turning out on the beach to

56
00:05:28,199 --> 00:05:35,597
rub their own hands and shake everybody else’s, and sing “Fill, fill!” A certain dark-complexioned

57
00:05:35,597 --> 00:05:41,199
Swab, however, who wouldn’t fill, or do anything else that was proposed to him,

58
00:05:41,199 --> 00:05:47,358
and whose heart was openly stated (by the boatswain) to be as black as his figure-head,

59
00:05:47,358 --> 00:05:54,597
proposed to two other Swabs to get all mankind into difficulties; which was so effectually done

60
00:05:54,597 --> 00:05:59,957
(the Swab family having considerable political influence) that it took half the evening to set

61
00:05:59,957 --> 00:06:05,879
things right, and then it was only brought about through an honest little grocer with a white hat,

62
00:06:05,879 --> 00:06:13,519
black gaiters, and red nose, getting into a clock, with a gridiron, and listening, and coming out,

63
00:06:13,519 --> 00:06:18,796
and knocking everybody down from behind with the gridiron whom he couldn’t confute with what he

64
00:06:18,796 --> 00:06:25,796
had overheard. This led to Mr. Wopsle’s (who had never been heard of before) coming in with a star

65
00:06:25,796 --> 00:06:32,398
and garter on, as a plenipotentiary of great power direct from the Admiralty, to say that the Swabs

66
00:06:32,398 --> 00:06:37,756
were all to go to prison on the spot, and that he had brought the boatswain down the Union Jack,

67
00:06:37,758 --> 00:06:45,038
as a slight acknowledgment of his public services. The boatswain, unmanned for the first time,

68
00:06:45,038 --> 00:06:50,839
respectfully dried his eyes on the Jack, and then cheering up, and addressing Mr. Wopsle

69
00:06:50,839 --> 00:06:58,519
as Your Honour, solicited permission to take him by the fin. Mr. Wopsle, conceding his fin with a

70
00:06:58,519 --> 00:07:05,160
gracious dignity, was immediately shoved into a dusty corner, while everybody danced a hornpipe;

71
00:07:05,160 --> 00:07:11,758
and from that corner, surveying the public with a discontented eye, became aware of me.

72
00:07:11,758 --> 00:07:17,398
The second piece was the last new grand comic Christmas pantomime, in the first scene of which,

73
00:07:17,398 --> 00:07:22,718
it pained me to suspect that I detected Mr. Wopsle with red worsted legs under a

74
00:07:22,718 --> 00:07:28,038
highly magnified phosphoric countenance and a shock of red curtain-fringe for his hair,

75
00:07:28,038 --> 00:07:33,358
engaged in the manufacture of thunderbolts in a mine, and displaying great cowardice when his

76
00:07:33,358 --> 00:07:39,557
gigantic master came home (very hoarse) to dinner. But he presently presented himself under worthier

77
00:07:39,559 --> 00:07:45,718
circumstances; for, the Genius of Youthful Love being in want of assistance,—on account

78
00:07:45,718 --> 00:07:51,358
of the parental brutality of an ignorant farmer who opposed the choice of his daughter’s heart,

79
00:07:51,358 --> 00:07:55,319
by purposely falling upon the object, in a flour-sack, out of the first-floor

80
00:07:55,319 --> 00:08:02,839
window,—summoned a sententious Enchanter; and he, coming up from the antipodes rather unsteadily,

81
00:08:02,839 --> 00:08:09,319
after an apparently violent journey, proved to be Mr. Wopsle in a high-crowned hat,

82
00:08:09,319 --> 00:08:15,519
with a necromantic work in one volume under his arm. The business of this enchanter on

83
00:08:15,519 --> 00:08:21,160
earth being principally to be talked at, sung at, butted at, danced at, and flashed at with

84
00:08:21,160 --> 00:08:26,957
fires of various colours, he had a good deal of time on his hands. And I observed,

85
00:08:26,957 --> 00:08:33,319
with great surprise, that he devoted it to staring in my direction as if he were lost in amazement.

86
00:08:33,319 --> 00:08:37,519
There was something so remarkable in the increasing glare of Mr. Wopsle’s eye,

87
00:08:37,519 --> 00:08:42,116
and he seemed to be turning so many things over in his mind and to grow so confused,

88
00:08:42,116 --> 00:08:47,199
that I could not make it out. I sat thinking of it long after he had ascended to the clouds

89
00:08:47,199 --> 00:08:53,199
in a large watch-case, and still I could not make it out. I was still thinking of it when

90
00:08:53,199 --> 00:08:58,957
I came out of the theatre an hour afterwards, and found him waiting for me near the door.

91
00:08:58,957 --> 00:09:02,358
“How do you do?” said I, shaking hands with him as we turned down

92
00:09:02,358 --> 00:09:05,957
the street together. “I saw that you saw me.”

93
00:09:05,957 --> 00:09:12,038
“Saw you, Mr. Pip!” he returned. “Yes, of course I saw you. But who else was there?”

94
00:09:12,038 --> 00:09:13,718
“Who else?”

95
00:09:13,718 --> 00:09:17,160
“It is the strangest thing,” said Mr. Wopsle,

96
00:09:17,160 --> 00:09:23,000
drifting into his lost look again; “and yet I could swear to him.”

97
00:09:23,000 --> 00:09:27,796
Becoming alarmed, I entreated Mr. Wopsle to explain his meaning.

98
00:09:27,796 --> 00:09:32,199
“Whether I should have noticed him at first but for your being there,” said Mr. Wopsle,

99
00:09:32,199 --> 00:09:37,597
going on in the same lost way, “I can’t be positive; yet I think I should.”

100
00:09:37,597 --> 00:09:42,078
Involuntarily I looked round me, as I was accustomed to look round me when I went home;

101
00:09:42,078 --> 00:09:45,358
for these mysterious words gave me a chill.

102
00:09:45,358 --> 00:09:52,639
“Oh! He can’t be in sight,” said Mr. Wopsle. “He went out before I went off. I saw him go.”

103
00:09:52,641 --> 00:09:55,398
Having the reason that I had for being suspicious,

104
00:09:55,398 --> 00:10:00,237
I even suspected this poor actor. I mistrusted a design to entrap me into

105
00:10:00,237 --> 00:10:07,237
some admission. Therefore I glanced at him as we walked on together, but said nothing.

106
00:10:07,237 --> 00:10:10,678
“I had a ridiculous fancy that he must be with you, Mr. Pip,

107
00:10:10,680 --> 00:10:17,237
till I saw that you were quite unconscious of him, sitting behind you there like a ghost.”

108
00:10:17,237 --> 00:10:22,199
My former chill crept over me again, but I was resolved not to speak yet,

109
00:10:22,199 --> 00:10:27,199
for it was quite consistent with his words that he might be set on to induce me to connect these

110
00:10:27,199 --> 00:10:34,639
references with Provis. Of course, I was perfectly sure and safe that Provis had not been there.

111
00:10:34,641 --> 00:10:41,038
“I dare say you wonder at me, Mr. Pip; indeed, I see you do. But it is so very strange! You’ll

112
00:10:41,038 --> 00:10:47,078
hardly believe what I am going to tell you. I could hardly believe it myself, if you told me.”

113
00:10:47,078 --> 00:10:48,758
“Indeed?” said I.

114
00:10:48,758 --> 00:10:54,000
“No, indeed. Mr. Pip, you remember in old times a certain Christmas Day,

115
00:10:54,000 --> 00:10:57,758
when you were quite a child, and I dined at Gargery’s,

116
00:10:57,758 --> 00:11:01,879
and some soldiers came to the door to get a pair of handcuffs mended?”

117
00:11:01,879 --> 00:11:04,358
“I remember it very well.”

118
00:11:04,358 --> 00:11:08,917
“And you remember that there was a chase after two convicts, and that we joined in it,

119
00:11:08,917 --> 00:11:12,559
and that Gargery took you on his back, and that I took the lead,

120
00:11:12,559 --> 00:11:15,879
and you kept up with me as well as you could?”

121
00:11:15,879 --> 00:11:22,438
“I remember it all very well.” Better than he thought,—except the last clause.

122
00:11:22,438 --> 00:11:25,879
“And you remember that we came up with the two in a ditch,

123
00:11:25,879 --> 00:11:27,879
and that there was a scuffle between them,

124
00:11:27,879 --> 00:11:33,480
and that one of them had been severely handled and much mauled about the face by the other?”

125
00:11:33,480 --> 00:11:35,756
“I see it all before me.”

126
00:11:35,758 --> 00:11:39,519
“And that the soldiers lighted torches, and put the two in the centre,

127
00:11:39,519 --> 00:11:43,277
and that we went on to see the last of them, over the black marshes,

128
00:11:43,277 --> 00:11:49,038
with the torchlight shining on their faces,—I am particular about that,—with the torchlight

129
00:11:49,038 --> 00:11:55,000
shining on their faces, when there was an outer ring of dark night all about us?”

130
00:11:55,000 --> 00:11:58,480
“Yes,” said I. “I remember all that.”

131
00:11:58,480 --> 00:12:04,756
“Then, Mr. Pip, one of those two prisoners sat behind you tonight. I saw him over your shoulder.”

132
00:12:04,758 --> 00:12:11,519
“Steady!” I thought. I asked him then, “Which of the two do you suppose you saw?”

133
00:12:11,519 --> 00:12:16,957
“The one who had been mauled,” he answered readily, “and I’ll swear I saw him!

134
00:12:16,957 --> 00:12:21,319
The more I think of him, the more certain I am of him.”

135
00:12:21,319 --> 00:12:24,957
“This is very curious!” said I, with the best assumption I

136
00:12:24,957 --> 00:12:29,839
could put on of its being nothing more to me. “Very curious indeed!”

137
00:12:29,839 --> 00:12:35,641
I cannot exaggerate the enhanced disquiet into which this conversation threw me, or the special

138
00:12:35,641 --> 00:12:42,559
and peculiar terror I felt at Compeyson’s having been behind me “like a ghost.” For if he had ever

139
00:12:42,559 --> 00:12:48,519
been out of my thoughts for a few moments together since the hiding had begun, it was in those very

140
00:12:48,519 --> 00:12:54,597
moments when he was closest to me; and to think that I should be so unconscious and off my guard

141
00:12:54,597 --> 00:13:01,319
after all my care was as if I had shut an avenue of a hundred doors to keep him out, and then had

142
00:13:01,319 --> 00:13:08,678
found him at my elbow. I could not doubt, either, that he was there, because I was there, and that,

143
00:13:08,680 --> 00:13:15,796
however slight an appearance of danger there might be about us, danger was always near and active.

144
00:13:15,796 --> 00:13:22,718
I put such questions to Mr. Wopsle as, When did the man come in? He could not tell me that;

145
00:13:22,718 --> 00:13:29,559
he saw me, and over my shoulder he saw the man. It was not until he had seen him for some time that

146
00:13:29,559 --> 00:13:35,438
he began to identify him; but he had from the first vaguely associated him with me, and known

147
00:13:35,438 --> 00:13:42,839
him as somehow belonging to me in the old village time. How was he dressed? Prosperously, but not

148
00:13:42,839 --> 00:13:50,796
noticeably otherwise; he thought, in black. Was his face at all disfigured? No, he believed not.

149
00:13:50,796 --> 00:13:55,796
I believed not too, for, although in my brooding state I had taken no especial notice of the people

150
00:13:55,796 --> 00:14:02,277
behind me, I thought it likely that a face at all disfigured would have attracted my attention.

151
00:14:02,277 --> 00:14:07,038
When Mr. Wopsle had imparted to me all that he could recall or I extract,

152
00:14:07,038 --> 00:14:12,358
and when I had treated him to a little appropriate refreshment, after the fatigues of the evening,

153
00:14:12,358 --> 00:14:19,639
we parted. It was between twelve and one o’clock when I reached the Temple,

154
00:14:19,641 --> 00:14:26,038
and the gates were shut. No one was near me when I went in and went home.

155
00:14:26,038 --> 00:14:31,917
Herbert had come in, and we held a very serious council by the fire. But there was nothing to

156
00:14:31,917 --> 00:14:37,796
be done, saving to communicate to Wemmick what I had that night found out, and to remind him that

157
00:14:37,796 --> 00:14:43,957
we waited for his hint. As I thought that I might compromise him if I went too often to the Castle,

158
00:14:43,957 --> 00:14:50,277
I made this communication by letter. I wrote it before I went to bed, and went out and posted it;

159
00:14:50,277 --> 00:14:55,358
and again no one was near me. Herbert and I agreed that we could do nothing else but be very

160
00:14:55,358 --> 00:15:02,078
cautious. And we were very cautious indeed,—more cautious than before, if that were possible,—and

161
00:15:02,078 --> 00:15:07,319
I for my part never went near Millstone Basin, except when I rowed by, and then I only looked at

162
00:15:07,319 --> 00:15:10,000
Mill Pond Bank as I looked at anything else. Chapter 48.
